# CEO Brief — Quiet-by-Design Standards for MWAA Terminals

*Author: airport-ceo research agent. Stage 1 independent brief. This is analysis; the JSONL companion carries the claim-level evidence.*

The Strategist has asked what a common quiet-by-design terminal standard would cost and what it would buy. I answer from the chair I hold — the seat that owns the balance sheet, the airline agreement, the board, and the political stack — not the seat that owns the paging microphone. A CEO who solves a passenger-experience irritant but loses the next rating review, or the next Majority-in-Interest vote, or the confidence of two governors and a mayor, has lost. So the question I run this thesis against is not "will it be quieter?" It is: does adopting this standard, in this form, at this moment, improve or degrade MWAA's long-term financial and political position?

## 1. Key findings a board chair can absorb in one read

- **This is a design-manual decision, not a program decision, and the difference matters.** If we scope the standard as acoustic and information-system *performance requirements* embedded in the MWAA Design Manual and consultant scopes, it is an executive act; it does not require a Board resolution, does not trigger Majority-in-Interest (MII) consultation, and does not surface to Moody's, S&P, or Fitch as a credit event. If we scope it as an *operating policy* that changes announcement content in airline-controlled hold rooms or reprograms tenant paging rights, it becomes an airline-relations negotiation on top of the $20B+ Dulles program we just announced. Same idea, entirely different political cost.

- **The window is now, because Concourse E opens in September 2026 and the $20B+ Dulles transformation was announced July 29, 2026.** Standards baked into program-level briefs before schematic design are 5–15x cheaper than retrofit — this is the least controversial economics in airport capital delivery. If we let the HOK Tier-2/Tier-3 scoping harden without an acoustic and PA-zoning specification, we are choosing to spend 2028 dollars to fix a 2026 omission.

- **MWAA carries ~$4.9B in outstanding revenue debt and plans to issue ~$5.5B more through 2028; net DSCR is projected to fall to ~1.3x from 1.63x in 2024.** [Source: https://www.bondbuyer.com/news/mwaa-goes-to-market] A quiet-by-design standard, correctly scoped, is neutral-to-positive for the credit story — it is exactly the kind of capital-discipline artifact rating analysts reward. Scoped badly, it becomes a rework line item in official statements.

- **United carries ~70% of IAD's passenger traffic and is now a formal partner in the Dulles announcement.** [Source: https://www.mwaa.com/news/airports-authority-and-united-airlines-execute-long-term-lease-dulles-international] Fitch groups airports at ~30%, 30–60%, and >60% single-carrier shares; IAD sits in the top band. [Source: https://dwuconsulting.com/dwu-ai/airport-bond-ratings] Any standard that touches United's operating envelope on domestic and international gates is an airline-relations decision first and a design decision second.

- **The 2025–2039 Airport Use Agreement is the commercial envelope, and MII carve-outs typically exclude safety- and security-driven and grant-funded work.** [Source: https://www.faa.gov/sites/faa.gov/files/airports/aip/airport-business-practices-and-their-impact-on-airline-competition.pdf] If we anchor the mandatory floor of the standard to NFPA 72 Chapter 24 voice-intelligibility compliance and ADA/ACAA accessibility, most of the mandatory content sits in exempt categories. That is the design move that keeps this off the MII agenda.

- **Federal partners impose most of the hard floors we would have to specify anyway.** NFPA 72 Chapter 24 sets Speech Transmission Index (STI) targets of ≥0.45 at 90% of measurement points and ≥0.50 average [Source: https://firemiddleeastmag.com/firedetection-alarms/voice-of-safety/]. The ACAA final rule effective January 16, 2025 tightens obligations for accessible communication with disabled passengers [Source: https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2024/12/17/2024-29731/ensuring-safe-accommodations-for-air-travelers-with-disabilities-using-wheelchairs]. Congress declined to raise the PFC cap in the 2024 FAA Reauthorization; PFC remains $4.50 [Source: https://transportationtodaynews.com/news/5247-not-everyones-board-proposal-raise-passenger-facility-charge-cap/]. A quiet-by-design standard makes explicit what federal law already implicitly requires — and that is a defensible posture in a rating briefing and a Congressional hearing alike.

- **The 17-member Board (7 VA, 4 DC, 3 MD, 3 Presidential; six-year terms, one reappointment) rewards initiatives that survive chair transitions.** [Source: https://law.lis.virginia.gov/authorities/metropolitan-washington-airports-authority/] Design manuals do. Signature programs frequently do not. A quiet-by-design standard is exactly the class of durable governance artifact that outlives its sponsor — which is precisely why it is a CEO's job, not a director's.

- **The one thing that would sink this: overreach.** If we conflate the design standard with an announcement-governance policy that mutes airline paging, we hand United and American an issue they do not currently have. From the chair, the discipline is to write the tightest standard that gets the operational benefit and not one line more.

## 2. Evidence

### 2.1 The bond-rating lens

MWAA's May 2025 assignments were Fitch AA- (Stable), Moody's Aa3 (Stable), and S&P AA- (Stable) on the Series 2025A airport system revenue bonds [Source: https://dwuconsulting.com/info/data/credit-ratings]. Fitch's rationale cited the dual-hub system's resilience, a well-balanced system-wide carrier mix (a system-level judgment that softens IAD's ~70% United concentration when the two airports are read together), capital progression at both airports, and a stable financial profile [Source: https://www.bondbuyer.com/news/mwaa-coming-to-market-with-829-million-municipal-bond-deal]. The forward look is where this thesis lives. Between 2025 and 2028, MWAA plans ~$5.5B of new debt; adjusted debt per O&D enplanement is projected to peak near $400 in 2028 and net DSCR to compress from 1.63x (2024) toward ~1.3x [Source: https://www.bondbuyer.com/news/mwaa-goes-to-market]. The rate covenant is 1.25x; the headroom on the next issuances is real but thinner than the current ratings suggest.

Moody's U.S. Publicly Managed Airports methodology weights Market Position most heavily, with smaller weights on Service Offering and Financial Metrics [Source: https://ma.moodys.com/rs/961-KCJ-308/images/PBM_1401434.pdf]. Within financial metrics: DSCR, days cash on hand, cost per enplanement, use ratios. Aa3+ airports averaged ~72 days cash on hand in 2024 medians [Source: https://dwuconsulting.com/dwu-ai/airport-bond-ratings]. What matters for this thesis: rating analysts do not directly evaluate acoustic performance, but they scrutinize (a) capital plan discipline, (b) airline-relations stability, and (c) evidence of program-management maturity. A published, cost-quantified design standard signals discipline. Retrofitting an unspecified performance requirement into a $20B+ program mid-build signals the opposite.

The all-in cost impact of getting this wrong: a 5–10 basis point spread widening on $1B of AMT bonds is $500K–$1M per year in additional debt service — small in isolation, meaningful across a five-issuance run. The point is not that quiet-by-design would move ratings. It is that governance artifacts that reduce program-execution risk have compounding value across a decade of issuance.

### 2.2 Airline use-and-lease structure

The 15-year Airport Use Agreement and Premises Lease took effect January 1, 2025 and runs through 2039 [Source: https://www.mwaa.com/news/airports-authority-and-united-airlines-execute-long-term-lease-dulles-international]. MII clauses in airline-airport use agreements typically require dual majorities — by number of signatory airlines and by activity (landed weight or enplanements) — to approve or disapprove capital projects funded through the airline rate base [Source: https://www.lawinsider.com/clause/majority-in-interest-of-airlines-mii]. Standard carve-outs exempt safety, security, and grant-funded projects [Source: https://www.faa.gov/sites/faa.gov/files/airports/aip/airport-business-practices-and-their-impact-on-airline-competition.pdf]. The exempt categories are where the mandatory floor of this standard should live.

The leverage question: United ~70% at IAD; American is the largest slot-holder at DCA. Either can, in principle, drive an MII outcome at its home airport. The July 29, 2026 Dulles announcement — a joint MWAA/United/USDOT plan roughly three times the size of the 2024 Board-adopted program [Source: https://www.airwaysmag.com/new-post/dulles-20-billion-overhaul-mobile-lounges] — has moved United from consulted signatory to formal program partner. The delicate implication: any MWAA-authored standard perceived as constraining United's gate-area operations arrives in a strengthened counterparty environment. The right posture is to write the standard as a set of performance criteria the airlines' own consultants will implement inside their premises to a common floor, not as a policy that reaches inside their operations.

### 2.3 The federal stack

Three federal partners set the floors this standard has to clear. NFPA 72 Chapter 24 (adopted through Virginia and DC building codes) governs emergency-communications intelligibility: STI ≥0.45 at 90% of ADS measurement points and ≥0.50 average [Source: https://firemiddleeastmag.com/firedetection-alarms/voice-of-safety/]; a "listen test" is nominally acceptable but the STI thresholds are the defensible measurement. The ACAA 2024 final rule effective January 16, 2025 codified accessible-communication training and notification obligations [Source: https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2024/12/17/2024-29731/ensuring-safe-accommodations-for-air-travelers-with-disabilities-using-wheelchairs]. FAA administers AIP and BIL grants — Concourse E received a $41M federal grant in May 2026 — and the PFC cap remains $4.50 after the 2024 Reauthorization [Source: https://transportationtodaynews.com/news/5247-not-everyones-board-proposal-raise-passenger-facility-charge-cap/]. TSA and CBP set additional communication-content and layout constraints in their zones.

Federal grant conditions and financial-reporting obligations are also tightening: GAO-26-107938 (April 2026) found FAA's internal controls over airport financial reporting inadequate, which foreshadows more prescriptive federal grant compliance in the next cycle [Source: https://files.gao.gov/reports/GAO-26-107938/index.html]. From the chair: this is a federal environment where documenting more of what we already do is prudent. A published standard is a compliance asset.

### 2.4 Board governance

The MWAA Board is a 17-member body: seven appointed by the Governor of Virginia (with General Assembly confirmation), four by the Mayor of the District of Columbia (with D.C. Council confirmation), three by the Governor of Maryland, and three by the President (with Senate advice and consent). Terms are six years; members can be reappointed once. Presidential appointees must be registered voters from states other than VA, MD, or DC, with no more than two of the same party [Source: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metropolitan_Washington_Airports_Authority]. The 2026 leadership is Chair Mark Uncapher, Vice Chair Alex Vogel, Secretary Felice C. Smith.

The governance implication for this thesis: a design standard issued through the Design Manual by the President and CEO is executive action; a resolution changing airline premises, capital cost, or tenant obligations goes to the Board. Four appointing bodies with staggered six-year terms means the chair changes; the design manual does not. Choose the vehicle that outlives the chair.

MWAA's structure — an independent public body created by interstate compact operating under a federal lease through 2067 — sits between an operating authority and a federal instrumentality. It is neither a city department (which would face annual appropriation politics) nor a bare-bones airport district (which would have narrower authority). That structural independence is the reason MWAA can adopt substantive design standards on an executive timeline without a ratepayer plebiscite. It is also why every standard we publish is scrutinized as if MWAA speaks for the federal government to some readers and for two states plus DC to others.

### 2.5 The host region

The political economy: Virginia's Governor holds seven of seventeen votes and confirms them through the General Assembly, which gives Richmond structural influence over any capital or standard decision. The Trump Administration's July 29, 2026 endorsement of the Dulles transformation gave the program federal political air cover — which cuts both ways [Source: https://www.transportation.gov/briefing-room/prepare-takeoff-president-donald-j-trump-us-transportation-secretary-sean-p-duffy]. Business-community allies (Chamber, the Northern Virginia tech and government-services cluster, the international-service constituency at IAD) are broadly aligned with any modernization narrative. Organized opposition to a quiet-by-design standard specifically is essentially zero — nobody rallies against acoustic performance criteria. The political risk is not opposition to the standard; it is opposition to its byproducts (announcement-content policy, tenant-media constraints) if we scope it too widely.

DCA carries a different political weight: it is the members-of-Congress airport. The 2024 FAA Reauthorization added 10 slot exemptions at DCA over strong regional opposition [Source: https://transportationtodaynews.com/news/5247-not-everyones-board-proposal-raise-passenger-facility-charge-cap/]. Any DCA standard that arguably makes the terminal feel more contested — quieter but visibly more surveilled by digital screens, for example — will find a Senator on it within a week. Design the DCA implementation to be visibly *calmer* rather than visibly *retrofitted*.

### 2.6 The CEO's calendar as evidence

I do not run the AOC. Peer large-hub CEOs in 2025–2026 are spending their calendar on four things: airline agreement renegotiation (or its aftermath); federal advocacy on PFC, BIL successors, and CBP staffing; capital-program financial packaging and rating-agency management; and board-and-region politics. Operations time is deputy-level. A design standard is precisely the class of decision that requires 20 hours of my calendar (governance vehicle, airline read, Board briefing, external comms) and then executes on the design leadership's calendar. That is a favorable ratio. Retrofit remediation, by contrast, is a calendar catastrophe — I have seen peers spend 100 hours on a single terminal-acoustics remediation that a $2M pre-schematic specification would have prevented.

### 2.7 Long-tenure strategic patterns, 2000–2025

Three patterns I have watched large-hub CEOs get right: (a) publishing performance-based design standards early and holding to them (Atlanta's terminal standardization, Dallas's Central Terminal Area consistency); (b) locking in long airline agreements before demand peaks (SFO 2011, LAX 2018); (c) using capital-program governance artifacts — program manuals, standards, delivery playbooks — as durable political capital across chair transitions.

Three that were "obvious at the time" and turned out wrong: (a) automated baggage systems as showcase infrastructure (Denver's system spiraled from $193M to more than $400M, delayed opening 16 months, and was largely abandoned) [Source: https://medium.com/@ketan.keshav7/the-400-million-lesson-what-the-denver-airport-baggage-fiasco-taught-us-about-escalation-and-c6f0bbc37305]; (b) hub over-scoping to a single airline's stated network plans in the year before a downturn (Pittsburgh post-USAir, Cincinnati post-Delta, Memphis post-Northwest); (c) treating passenger-experience aspirations as design goals rather than as measurable performance requirements — the "calming environment" trap, which produces expensive finishes and no acoustic outcome.

The pattern under all three failures is the same: soft goals, hard money. The pattern under the three successes is also the same: hard specifications, patient execution, durable governance vehicles.

## 3. The financial-and-political case, as the CEO sees it

**For (adopt as a tiered design-manual standard, mandatory floor narrow, context-dependent options wide):** Cheap political capital now, expensive retrofit avoided later. Neutral-to-positive on the credit story because it demonstrates capital discipline in the middle of the largest issuance window in MWAA history. Compliance-forward posture with FAA, DOT, and ACAA. Executive-authority vehicle avoids MII exposure and Board fatigue. Outlives the chair, which four-appointing-body governance rewards. Aligns with the political geometry of the Dulles announcement without conceding any of the standard-setting authority MWAA holds under the compact.

**Against (adopt as a system-wide operational policy that touches announcement content and airline-controlled audio):** Triggers MII consultation on the airline premises side, arriving as a "second ask" on top of a $20B+ program United has just committed to. Invites accessibility-community and Congressional oversight over anything that could be read as reducing communication. Creates a media narrative — "MWAA silences DCA" — that a single Senator can activate in 48 hours. Consumes CEO calendar that should go to financing the Dulles program. Fails the long-tenure test: aspirational, subjective, and easy to reverse.

**My read from the chair:** Adopt narrow, embed early, publish clearly, and stop. The standard is a design-manual instrument. The mandatory floor is acoustic performance criteria in landside and common areas, PA-zoning discipline, NFPA 72 Chapter 24 compliance, ACAA-consistent multimodal communication, and post-occupancy verification. Everything else is recommended or context-dependent, with IAD and DCA implementations differing because the buildings, the passenger profiles, and the historic-preservation constraints differ. The standard does not tell an airline what to announce in its hold room. If we hold that line, this is one of the highest-return governance artifacts available to us this decade.

## 4. Data points and precedents a strategist can cite

1. **MWAA credit posture and issuance runway:** Aa3/AA-/AA- as of May 2025; ~$4.9B outstanding; ~$5.5B planned through 2028; net DSCR projected to fall from 1.63x (2024) toward ~1.3x; rate covenant 1.25x; CPE projected to $20.71 by 2030 (pre-July 2026 Dulles announcement) [Source: https://www.bondbuyer.com/news/mwaa-goes-to-market].

2. **Rating-agency financial-metric framework:** Moody's Aa3+ airport median ~72 days cash on hand (2024); 1.25x DSCR is the predominant senior-lien minimum; Fitch tiers single-carrier concentration at ~30%, 30–60%, and >60% (IAD sits in the top band) [Source: https://dwuconsulting.com/dwu-ai/airport-bond-ratings].

3. **NFPA 72 STI thresholds as the defensible mandatory floor:** ≥0.45 at 90% of ADS measurement points; ≥0.50 average [Source: https://firemiddleeastmag.com/firedetection-alarms/voice-of-safety/]. Federal accessibility floor: ACAA 2024 final rule effective January 16, 2025 [Source: https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2024/12/17/2024-29731/ensuring-safe-accommodations-for-air-travelers-with-disabilities-using-wheelchairs].

4. **Large-hub CPE range for CFO benchmarking:** FY2024 large-hub CPE ranged $3.93 (ATL) to $36.01 (JFK), median ~$12.88 [Source: https://dwuconsulting.com/info/data/cpe]. MWAA's projected $20.71 by 2030 already sits in the upper quartile; every avoidable retrofit is a CPE line item.

5. **Precedent — the cost of "obvious at the time":** Denver's automated baggage system grew from $193M to more than $400M and delayed opening 16 months [Source: https://medium.com/@ketan.keshav7/the-400-million-lesson-what-the-denver-airport-baggage-fiasco-taught-us-about-escalation-and-c6f0bbc37305]. The applicable lesson is not baggage; it is that showcase infrastructure specified late in a large-hub program is the single most reliable source of cost overruns in the industry.
